var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Pöschl-Teller-Potential","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"The Pöschl-Teller potential is one of the few potentials for which the quantum mechanical Schrödinger equation has an analytical solution. It has a finite number of bound states, which can be inferred easily from its potential strength parameter lambda. It is defined for one-dimensional systems.","title":"Pöschl-Teller Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Definitions","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Potential","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Maximum-Quantum-Number","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Maximum Quantum Number"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Eigenvalues","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Associated-Legendre-Polynomials","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"","title":"Associated Legendre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction(), potential potential() and n_max() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by PoschlTeller and the parameters lambda, m, hbar, x_0.\n\nusing Antique\nPT = PoschlTeller(lambda=4, m=1.0, hbar=1.0, x_0=1.0)\n; # hide\n\nParameters:\n\nPT.lambda\nPT.m\nPT.hbar\nPT.x_0\n\nMaximum quantum number:\n\nn_max(PT)\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(PT, n=0)\nenergy(PT, n=1)\nenergy(PT, n=2)\nenergy(PT, n=3)\n\nPotential energy curve:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x)$\")\nlines!(ax, -6..6, x -> potential(PT, x))\nf\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# setting\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(x)$\")\n\n# plot\nw0 = lines!(ax, -3..3, x -> wavefunction(PT, x, n=0))\nw1 = lines!(ax, -3..3, x -> wavefunction(PT, x, n=1))\nw2 = lines!(ax, -3..3, x -> wavefunction(PT, x, n=2))\nw3 = lines!(ax, -3..3, x -> wavefunction(PT, x, n=3))\n\n# legend\naxislegend(ax, [w0, w1, w2, w3], [L\"n=0\", L\"n=1\", L\"n=2\", L\"n=3\"], position=:lb)\n\nf\n\nPotential energy curve, Energy levels, Wave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x),~E_n,~\\psi_n(x) \\times 5 + E_n$\", aspect=1, limits=(-4,4,-10.5,1))\n# hidespines!(ax)\n# hidedecorations!(ax)\n\nfor n in 0:3\n  # classical turning point\n  xE = acosh(sqrt(PT.lambda*(PT.lambda+1)/abs(energy(PT,n=n))/2))\n  # energy\n  hlines!(ax, energy(PT, n=n), color=:black, linewidth=1, linestyle=:dash)\n  lines!(ax, [-xE,xE], fill(energy(PT,n=n),2), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n  # wave function\n  lines!(ax, -4..4, x -> energy(PT,n=n) + wavefunction(PT,x,n=n), linewidth=2)\nend\n\n#potential\nlines!(ax, -4..4, x -> potential(PT,x), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/PoschlTeller.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"PoschlTeller/#Testing","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/PoschlTeller.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.PoschlTellers.PoschlTeller-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH =\n  - frachbar^22 m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2\n  - frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(xx_0)\n\nAfter introducing the dimensionless variables\n\n  x^ast equiv xx_0qquad E^ast equiv frachbar^2m x_0^2 E\n\nthe Schrödinger equation reduces to\n\n  hatH^ast psi(x^ast) = E^ast psi(x^ast)\n\nwith\n\n  hatH^ast = - frac12 fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx^ast^2 - fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(x^ast)\n\nParameters are specified within the following struct:\n\nPT = PoschlTeller(lambda=1, m=1.0, hbar=1.0, x_0=1.0)\n\nlambda determines the potential strength. Currently only integer values for lambda are supported.\n\nReferences\n\n[16] G. Pöschl, E. Teller, Zeitschrift für Physik, 83 (3–4), 143 (1933), (https://doi.org/10.1007%2FBF01331132). More general definitions are given as (2a), (2b) or (11)\n[17] S. Flügge, Practical Quantum Mechanics (Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 1999), (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61995-3) p.94 Problem 39. Potential hole of modified Poschl-Teller type\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.PoschlTellers.PoschlTeller"},{"category":"method","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.potential-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"potential(model::PoschlTeller, x)\n\nbeginaligned\nV(x)\n= -frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 mathrmsech^2(xx_0) \n= -frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(xx_0) \nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.n_max-Tuple{PoschlTeller}-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"n_max(model::PoschlTeller)\n\nnote: Note\nNote that the number of bound states n_max + 1 is not equal to the maximum quantum number n_max, since we count the ground state from n=0 in this model.\n\nn_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.n_max"},{"category":"method","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.energy-Tuple{PoschlTeller}-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"energy(model::PoschlTeller; n::Integer=0, nocheck=false)\n\nE_n = -frachbar^2m x_0^2fracmu^22\n\nwhere mu = mu(n) = n_mathrmmax-n+1, and n_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"wavefunction(model::PoschlTeller, x; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(x) = frac(-1)^μsqrtx_0 P_lambda^mu(mathrmtanh(xx_0)) sqrtmufracGamma(lambda-mu+1)Gamma(lambda+mu+1)\n\nwhere mu = mu(n) = n_mathrmmax-n+1, and n_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1 and P_lambda^mu are the associated Legendre functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"PoschlTeller/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}-PoschlTeller","page":"Pöschl-Teller Potential","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::PoschlTeller, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nAssociated Legendre polynomials are the associated Legendre functions for integer indices. Here we use the same notation of the associated Legendre functions as in the model HydrogenAtom.\n\nbeginaligned\nP_n^m(x)\n= left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n= left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n= frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"D. J. Griffiths and D. F. Schroeter. Introduction to Quantum Mechanics. 3 Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018).\n\n\n\nJ. Branson. The Delta Function Potential, https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node154.html. Accessed: 2025-07-25.\n\n\n\nDLMF. The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, https://dlmf.nist.gov/. Accessed: 2025-08-08.\n\n\n\ncpprefjp. C++ Japanese Reference, https://cpprefjp.github.io/. Accessed: 2025-08-08.\n\n\n\nL. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz. Quantum Mechanics: Non-Relativistic Theory. 2 Edition (Pergamon Press, 1965).\n\n\n\nL. I. Schiff. Quantum Mechanics. 3 Edition (McGraw-Hill Book Company, 1968).\n\n\n\nA. Messiah. Quantum Mechanics (Dover Publications, 1999).\n\n\n\nW. Greiner. Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction. 3 Edition (Springer, 1994).\n\n\n\nD. J. Griffiths. Introduction to Quantum Mechanics (Prentice Hall, 1995).\n\n\n\nD. A. McQuarrie and J. D. Simon. Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach (University Science Books, 1997).\n\n\n\nP. W. Atkins and J. D. Paula. Atkins' Physical Chemistry. 8 Edition (W. H. Freeman, 2008).\n\n\n\nJ. J. Sakurai and J. Napolitano. Modern Quantum Mechanics. 3 Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2021).\n\n\n\nP. M. Morse. Diatomic Molecules According to the Wave Mechanics. II. Vibrational Levels. Phys. Rev. 34, 57–64 (1929).\n\n\n\nJ. P. Dahl and M. Springborg. The Morse oscillator in position space, momentum space, and phase space. J. Chem. Phys. 88, 4535–4547 (1988).\n\n\n\nW.-K. Shao, Y. He and J. Pan. Some identities for the generalized Laguerre polynomials. J. Nonlinear Sci. Appl. 9, 3388–3396 (2016).\n\n\n\nG. Pöschl and E. Teller. Bemerkungen zur Quantenmechanik des anharmonischen Oszillators. Zeitschrift für Physik 83, 143–151 (1933).\n\n\n\nS. Flügge. Practical Quantum Mechanics. 1 Edition (Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 1999).\n\n\n\nC. LibreTexts. The Energy Levels of a Rigid Rotor, https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Physical_Chemistry_(LibreTexts)/05%3A_The_Harmonic_Oscillator_and_the_Rigid_Rotor/5.08%3A_The_Energy_Levels_of_a_Rigid_Rotor. Accessed: 2025-08-08.\n\n\n\nUCSD. UCSD Physics 130, Quantum Physics, https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node244.html. Accessed: 2025-08-08.\n\n\n\nA. Messiah. Quantum Mechanics. Vol. 1 (North-Holland Publishing Company, 1961).\n\n\n\nW. Greiner. Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction (Springer, 2001).\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Infinite-Potential-Well-(Particle-in-a-Box)","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"The infinite potential well (particle in a box) is the simplest model for a quantum mechanical system.","title":"Infinite Potential Well (Particle in a Box)"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Definitions","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Potential","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Eigenvalues","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by InfinitePotentialWell and several parameters L, m and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nIPW = InfinitePotentialWell(L=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nIPW.L\nIPW.m\nIPW.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(IPW, n=1)\nenergy(IPW, n=2)\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(x)$\")\n\n# plot\nw1 = lines!(ax, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW, x, n=1))\nw2 = lines!(ax, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW, x, n=2))\nw3 = lines!(ax, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW, x, n=3))\nw4 = lines!(ax, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW, x, n=4))\nw5 = lines!(ax, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW, x, n=5))\n\n# legend\naxislegend(ax, [w1, w2, w3, w4, w5], [L\"n=1\", L\"n=2\", L\"n=3\", L\"n=4\", L\"n=5\"], position=:lb)\n\nf\n\nPotential energy curve, Energy levels, Wave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x),~E_n,~\\psi_n(x) \\times 5 + E_n$\", aspect=1, limits=(-0.5,1.5,0,140))\n# hidespines!(ax)\n# hidedecorations!(ax)\n\n# plot\nfor n in 1:5\n  # energy\n  lines!(ax, [0,IPW.L], fill(energy(IPW,n=n),2), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n  # wave function\n  lines!(ax, 0..IPW.L, x -> energy(IPW,n=n) + 5*wavefunction(IPW,x,n=n), linewidth=2)\nend\n\n#potential\nlines!(ax, [0,0,IPW.L,IPW.L], [140,0,0,140], color=:black, linewidth=2)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/InfinitePotentialWell.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Testing","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/InfinitePotentialWell.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Antique.InfinitePotentialWells.InfinitePotentialWell-InfinitePotentialWell","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 + V(x)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nIPW = InfinitePotentialWell(L=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nL is the length of the box, m is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.31, 2.2 THE INFINITE SQUARE WELL\n\nProofs\n\nEigen Functions & Eigen Values\nNormalization\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.InfinitePotentialWells.InfinitePotentialWell"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Antique.potential-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell, Any}-InfinitePotentialWell","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"potential(model::InfinitePotentialWell; x)\n\nV(x) =\nleft\n  beginarrayll\n  0       0 leq x leq L \n  infty  x lt 0 L lt x\n  endarray\nright\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Antique.energy-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell}-InfinitePotentialWell","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"energy(model::InfinitePotentialWell; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n = frachbar^2 n^2 pi^22 m L^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell, Any}-InfinitePotentialWell","page":"Infinite Potential Well","text":"wavefunction(model::InfinitePotentialWell, x; n::Integer=1)\n\npsi_n(x) = sqrtfrac2L sin fracnpi xL\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Spherical-Oscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"The spherical oscillator (3D isotropic harmonic oscillator) is the most frequently used model in quantum physics. This model uses a spherical coordinate system.","title":"Spherical Oscillator"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Definitions","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Potential","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Eigenvalues","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Radial-Functions","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Radial Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Generalized-Laguerre-Polynomials","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Generalized Laguerre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Spherical-Harmonics","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Spherical Harmonics"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Associated-Legendre-Polynomials","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"","title":"Associated Legendre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by SphericalOscillator and several parameters k, mu and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nSO = SphericalOscillator(k=1.0, mu=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; # hide\n\nParameters:\n\nSO.k\nSO.mu\nSO.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(SO, n=0)\nenergy(SO, n=1)\nenergy(SO, n=2)\nenergy(SO, n=0, l=1)\nenergy(SO, n=1, l=1)\nenergy(SO, n=2, l=1)","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Testing","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/SphericalOscillator.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.SphericalOscillators.SphericalOscillator-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + frac12 k r^2\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nSO = SphericalOscillator(k=1.0, mu=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nk is the force constant, μ is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[17] S. Flügge, Practical Quantum Mechanics (Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 1999), (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61995-3) [p.166, Problem 65. Spherical oscillator].\n[19] UCSD Physics 130, Quantum Physics, (https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node244.html)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.SphericalOscillators.SphericalOscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.potential-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"potential(model::SphericalOscillator, r)\n\nV(r)\n= frac12 k r^2\n= frac12 mu omega^2 r^2\n= frac12 hbar omega xi^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkmu is the angular frequency and xi = sqrtfracmuomegahbarr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.energy-Tuple{SphericalOscillator}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"energy(model::SphericalOscillator; n::Integer=0, l::Integer=0)\n\nE_nl\n= left(2n + l + frac32right)hbar omega\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkmu.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any, Any, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"wavefunction(model::SphericalOscillator, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=0, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"radial_function(model::SphericalOscillator, r; n=0, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = sqrt fracgamma^322sqrtpi sqrtfrac2^n+l+3 n(2n+2l+1) xi^l expleft(-xi^22right)L_n^(l+frac12) left(xi^2right)\n\nwhere gamma = muomegahbar and xi = sqrtgammar = sqrtmuomegahbarr are defined. The generalized Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^(alpha)(x) = fracx^-alpha mathrme^xn  fracmathrmd^nmathrmd x^nleft(mathrme^-x x^n+alpharight). The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::SphericalOscillator, x; n=0, α=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), not the associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_n^(alpha)(x)\n  = fracx^-alphae^xn fracd^ndx^nleft(x^n+alphae^-xright) \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k left(beginarrayl n+alpha  n-k endarrayright) fracx^kk  \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k fracGamma(alpha+n+1)Gamma(alpha+k+1)Gamma(n-k+1) fracx^kk \nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^(0)(x) = 1 \n  L_1^(0)(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^(1)(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^(0)(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(1)(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(2)(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^(0)(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(1)(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(2)(x) = 10 - 10 x + 52 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(3)(x) = 20 - 15 x + 3 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^(0)(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(1)(x) = 5 - 10 x + 5 x^2 - 56 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(2)(x) = 15 - 20 x + 152 x^2 - 1 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(3)(x) = 35 - 35 x + 212 x^2 - 76 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(4)(x) = 70 - 56 x + 14 x^2 - 43 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::SphericalOscillator, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"SphericalOscillator/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}-SphericalOscillator","page":"Spherical Oscillator","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::SphericalOscillator, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Delta-Potential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"The Delta potential is one of the simplest models for a quantum mechanical system in 1D. It always has one bound state and its wave function has a cusp at the origin.","title":"Delta Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Definitions","page":"Delta Potential","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Potential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Eigenvalues","page":"Delta Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Delta Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Delta Potential","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by DeltaPotential and several parameters alpha, m and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nDP = DeltaPotential(alpha=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nDP.alpha\nDP.m\nDP.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(DP)\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# setting\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(x)$\")\n\n# plot\nw = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(DP, x))\n\nf\n\nPotential energy curve, energy levels, wave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x),~E_n,~\\psi_n(x) \\times 5 + E_n$\", aspect=1, limits=(-5,5,-0.6,0.6))\n# hidespines!(ax)\n# hidedecorations!(ax)\n\n# energy\nhlines!(ax, energy(DP), color=:black, linewidth=1, linestyle=:dash)\n\n# wave function\nlines!(ax, -5..5, x -> energy(DP) + wavefunction(DP,x), linewidth=2)\n\n#potential\nlines!(ax, [-5,0,0,0,5], [0,0,-1,0,0], color=:black, linewidth=2)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/DeltaPotential.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"DeltaPotential/#Testing","page":"Delta Potential","text":"Unit testing and integration testing were done using numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/DeltaPotential.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"DeltaPotential/#Antique.DeltaPotentials.DeltaPotential-DeltaPotential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 - alpha delta(x)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nDP = DeltaPotential(alpha=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nalpha is the potential strength, m is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018), (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.63, 2.5.2 The Delta-Function Well\n[2] UCSD Physics 130, Quantum Physics, (https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node154.html)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.DeltaPotentials.DeltaPotential"},{"category":"method","location":"DeltaPotential/#Antique.potential-Tuple{DeltaPotential, Any}-DeltaPotential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"potential(model::DeltaPotential, x)\n\nV(x) = -alpha delta(x)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"DeltaPotential/#Antique.energy-Tuple{DeltaPotential}-DeltaPotential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"energy(model::DeltaPotential)\n\nE = - fracmalpha^22hbar^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"DeltaPotential/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{DeltaPotential, Any}-DeltaPotential","page":"Delta Potential","text":"wavefunction(model::DeltaPotential, x)\n\npsi(x) = fracsqrtmalphahbar mathrme^-malpha xhbar^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Coulomb-2-Body-System","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"This is the model of two particles interacting through Coulomb forces, such as positronium, muonium, hydrogen atoms, deuterium atoms, etc.","title":"Coulomb 2-Body System"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Definitions","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Potential","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Eigenvalues","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Radial-Functions","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Radial Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Associated-Laguerre-Polynomials","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Associated Laguerre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Spherical-Harmonics","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Spherical Harmonics"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Associated-Legendre-Polynomials","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"","title":"Associated Legendre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by CoulombTwoBody and several parameters z_1, z_2, m_1, m_2, m_e, a_0, E_h and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nPs = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=1, m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Parameters","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"Ps.z_1\nPs.z_2\nPs.m_1\nPs.m_2\nPs.m_e\nPs.a_0\nPs.E_h\nPs.hbar","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Eigenvalues-2","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"Example calculations for positronium:\n\nenergy(Ps, n=1)\nenergy(Ps, n=2)","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Mass-and-Charge-Dependence","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"The values of masses are cited from the 2018 CODATA recommended values, E. Tiesinga, et al., Rev. Mod. Phys. 93, 025010 (2021).\n\nme = 1.0           # me # \nm_mu = 206.7682830 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi%2Dbin/cuu/Value?mmusme\nmp = 1836.15267343 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi%2Dbin/cuu/Value?mpsme\nmd = 3670.48296788 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi%2Dbin/cuu/Value?mdsme\nmt = 5496.92153573 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi%2Dbin/cuu/Value?mtsme\nmh = 5495.88528007 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mhsme\nma = 7294.29954142 # me # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?malsme\n\nPs = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=me)\nMu = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=m_mu)\nH  = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=mp)\nD  = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=md)\nT  = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=mt)\nBO = CoulombTwoBody(m_1=me, m_2=Inf)\n\nHe3p = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=me, m_2=mh)\nHe4p = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=me, m_2=ma)\nHeInfp = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=me, m_2=Inf)\n\np_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=m_mu, m_2=mp)\nd_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=m_mu, m_2=md)\nt_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=m_mu, m_2=mt)\nb_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=m_mu, m_2=Inf)\nh_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=m_mu, m_2=mh)\nalpha_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=m_mu, m_2=ma)\n\nprintln(\"    \\tE / Eₕ\")\nprintln(\"Ps  \\t\", energy(Ps))\nprintln(\"Mu  \\t\", energy(Mu))\nprintln(\"H   \\t\", energy(H))\nprintln(\"D   \\t\", energy(D))\nprintln(\"T   \\t\", energy(T))\nprintln(\"∞H  \\t\", energy(BO))\nprintln(\"³He⁺\\t\", energy(He3p))\nprintln(\"⁴He⁺\\t\", energy(He4p))\nprintln(\"∞He⁺\\t\", energy(HeInfp))\nprintln(\"pμ  \\t\", energy(p_mu))\nprintln(\"dμ  \\t\", energy(d_mu))\nprintln(\"tμ  \\t\", energy(t_mu))\nprintln(\"bμ  \\t\", energy(b_mu))\nprintln(\"hμ  \\t\", energy(h_mu))\nprintln(\"αμ  \\t\", energy(alpha_mu))\n\nprintln(\"   \\t<δ³(r)> / a₀⁻³\")\nprintln(\"1/8π =\\t\", 1/8/π)\nprintln(\"Ps    \\t\", abs(wavefunction(Ps,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"Mu    \\t\", abs(wavefunction(Mu,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"H     \\t\", abs(wavefunction(H ,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"D     \\t\", abs(wavefunction(D ,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"T     \\t\", abs(wavefunction(T ,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"∞H    \\t\", abs(wavefunction(BO,0,0,0))^2)\nprintln(\"1/π = \\t\", 1/π)","title":"Mass and Charge Dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Lifetime-of-Positronium","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"The lifetime tau of positronium (Ps, mathrme^+mathrme^-) is written as\n\ntau = frac1Gamma\n\nGamma = 4 pi alpha^4 c a_0^2 langledelta^3(pmbr)rangle\n\nwhere langledelta^3(pmbr)rangle = langlepsidelta^3(pmbr)psirangle = psi(pmb0)^2 = frac18pi a_0^-3 simeq 2685times10^29mathrmm^-3 is the value of probability density at the origin (r=0).\n\n# (7.169) in D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Elementary Particles (John Wiley & Sons, Inc. 1987) ISBN 0-471-60386-4\n# S. Berko, H. N. Pendleton, Annual Review of Nuclear and Particle Science, 30, 543 (1980) https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.ns.30.120180.002551)\n# A. M. Frolov, S. I. Kryuchkov, and V. H. Smith, Jr., Phys. Rev. A, 51, 4514 (1995) https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.51.4514\n\nalpha = 7.2973525693e-3 #       # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?alph\nc   = 299792458         # m s-1 # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c\na0  = 5.29177210903e-11 # m     # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?bohrrada0\n\nPs = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=1, z_2=-1, m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\ndelta = abs(wavefunction(Ps,0,0,0))^2 * a0^(-3)\nGamma = 4 * pi * alpha^4 * c *  a0^2 * delta\ntau = 1/Gamma\nprintln(\"<δ> = \", abs(wavefunction(Ps,0,0,0))^2, \" a₀⁻³\")\nprintln(\"    = \", delta, \" m⁻³\")\nprintln(\"Γ   = \", Gamma / 1e9, \" GHz\")\nprintln(\"τ   = \", tau / 1e-12, \" ps\")","title":"Lifetime of Positronium"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Hyperfine-Splitting","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"The hyperfine splitting of hydrogen atoms is given as\n\nDelta E (mathrmH) = -frac23 mu_0 gamma_mathrmp gamma_mathrme hbar^2 langledelta^3(pmbr)rangle\n\nin Griffiths(1982). Note that this formula is not available for deuterium (D) and positronium (Ps) because of the different spin between the proton and the deuteron for D, and the contribution of positron-electron pair annihilation for Ps. And also note that the mass of proton is used for the definitions of gyromagnetic ratio in all nucleons and nuclei:\n\nbeginaligned\ngamma_mathrme   = frac-e2 m_mathrme g_mathrme     \ngamma_mathrme^+ = frac+e2 m_mathrme g_mathrme     \ngamma_mathrmmu = frac-e2 m_mathrmmu g_mathrmmu \ngamma_mathrmp   = frac+e2 m_mathrmp g_mathrmp     \ngamma_mathrmd   = frac+e2 m_mathrmp g_mathrmd     \ngamma_mathrmt   = frac+e2 m_mathrmp g_mathrmt     \ngamma_mathrmh   = frac+2e2 m_mathrmp g_mathrmh    \nendaligned\n\nThe value of the probability density at the origin is langledelta^3(pmbr)rangle = langlepsidelta^3(pmbr)psirangle = psi(pmb0)^2 simeq frac1pi a_0^-3 simeq 2148times10^30mathrmm^-3 in Mu, H, D and T. These values are very different in Ps, ^3mathrmHe^+ and muonic hydrogen (mathrmpmu) due to the difference of reduced masses and charges. The energy can be converted to a frequency (Hz) by v = Delta E  h.\n\na0 = 5.29177210903e-11   # m  # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?bohrrada0\nEh = 4.3597447222071e-18 # J  # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?hr\nhbar = 1.054571817e-34   # J s # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?hbar\n\nme = 9.1093837015e-31  # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?me\nm_mu = 1.883531627e-28 # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mmu\nmp = 1.67262192369e-27 # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mp\nmd = 3.3435837724e-27  # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?md\nmt = 5.0073567446e-27  # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mt\nmh = 5.0064127796e-27  # kg # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mh\n\ne  = 1.602176634e-19  # C      # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?e\nmu0 = 1.25663706212e-6 # N A-2  # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mu0\nh  = 6.62607015e-34   # J Hz-1 # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?h\neV = 1.602176634e-19  # J      # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?evj\n\nge = 2.00231930436256 # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gem\ng_mu = 2.0023318418   # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gmum\ngp = 5.5856946893     # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gp\ngd = 0.8574382338     # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gdn\ngt = 5.957924931      # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gtn\ngh = -4.255250615     # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?ghn\n\nPs = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=me, m_2=me, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\nMu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=me, m_2=m_mu, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\nH  = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=me, m_2=mp, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\nD  = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=me, m_2=md, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\nT  = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=me, m_2=mt, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\nhe = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+2, m_1=me, m_2=mh, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\np_mu = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=+1, m_1=m_mu, m_2=mp, m_e=me, a_0=a0, E_h=Eh, hbar=hbar)\n\ndE_H  = 2/3 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * gp / mp * abs(wavefunction(H,0,0,0))^2\ndE_D  =   1 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * gd / mp * abs(wavefunction(D,0,0,0))^2\ndE_T  = 2/3 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * gt / mp * abs(wavefunction(H,0,0,0))^2\ndE_Ps = 7/6 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * ge / me * abs(wavefunction(Ps,0,0,0))^2\ndE_Mu = 2/3 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * g_mu / m_mu * abs(wavefunction(Mu,0,0,0))^2\ndE_he = 2/3 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * ge / me * gh / mp * abs(wavefunction(he,0,0,0))^2\ndE_pmu = 2/3 / 4 * mu0 * hbar^2 * e^2 * g_mu / m_mu * gp / mp * abs(wavefunction(p_mu,0,0,0))^2\n\n# Karshenboim(2001) https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.hep-ph/0109128\n# Karshenboim(2003) https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.hep-ph/0310099\n# Griffiths(1982)   https://doi.org/10.1119/1.12733\n# Adamczak(2012)    https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nimb.2012.04.001\n\nprintln(\"H \\t\", dE_H  / h / 1e6,  \" MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"1420.405751768(1)  MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2001)\")\nprintln(\"D \\t\", dE_D  / h / 1e6,  \" MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"327.384352522(2)   MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2001)\")\nprintln(\"T \\t\", dE_T  / h / 1e6,  \" MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"1516.701470773(8)  MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2001)\")\nprintln(\"Ps\\t\", dE_Ps / h / 1e6, \"  MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"203391.7(6)        MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2003)\")\nprintln(\"Mu\\t\", dE_Mu / h / 1e6, \"  MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"4463.30278(5)      MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2001)\")\nprintln(\"³He⁺\\t\", dE_he / h / 1e6, \" MHz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", \"-8665.649867(10)   MHz\\t  Karshenboim(2001)\")\nprintln(\"µp\\t\", dE_pmu / h / 1e12, \"  THz\\t  Antique.jl + CODATA2018\")\nprintln(\"  \\t\", 0.182725*eV / h / 1e12 , \"  THz\\t  Griffiths(1982), Adamczak(2012)\")\n\n1S wave function of Ps:\n\nimport Antique\nPs = Antique.CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=1, m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n@show Antique.energy(Ps)\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(\n    size = (420,300),\n    fontsize = 11,\n    backgroundcolor = :transparent\n)\n\nax = Axis(\n    fig[1,1],\n    xlabel = L\"$r / a_0$\",\n    ylabel = L\"$\\psi(r) / a_0^{-3/2}$\",\n    ylabelsize = 16.5,\n    xlabelsize = 16.5,\n)\n\nlines!(ax, 0..10, r -> exp(-r/2)/sqrt(8π), label=\"exp(-r/2)/sqrt(8π)\")\nlines!(ax, 0..2, r -> (1-r/2)/sqrt(8π), label=\"(1-r/2)/sqrt(8π)\")\nlines!(ax, 0..10, r -> abs(Antique.wavefunction(Ps,r,0,0)), linestyle=:dash, color=:black, label=\"Antique.jl\")\n\naxislegend(ax, position=:rt, framevisible=false)\n\nfig","title":"Hyperfine Splitting"},{"category":"section","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Testing","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"Unit testing and integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/CoulombTwoBody.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.CoulombTwoBodies.CoulombTwoBody-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + fracz_1 z_2ra_0 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of particle 1 and particle 2. The potential includes only Coulomb interaction and it does not include fine or hyperfine interactions in this model. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nCTB = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=1, m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nz₁ is the charge number of particle 1,  z₂ is the charge number of particle 2,  m₁ is the mass of particle 1,  m₂ is the mass of particle 2, m_mathrme is the electron mass (use the same unit as m₁ and m₂. For example of hydrogen atom, use m_mathrme=91093837139times10^-31mathrmkg, m_1=91093837139times10^-31mathrmkg and m_2=167262192595times10^-27mathrmkg in the IS unit system, use m_mathrme=10m_mathrme, m_1=10m_mathrme and m_2=1836152673426m_mathrme in the atomic unit.), a_0 is the Bohr radius, E_mathrmh is the Hartree energy and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference (C++日本語リファレンス) cpprefjp, assoc_legendre, assoc_laguerre\n[20] A. Messiah, Quantum Mechanics VOLUME Ⅰ (North-Holland Publishing Company, 1961), p.412 I. THE HYDROGEN ATOM\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.143 4.2 THE HYDROGEN ATOM, p.200 Problem 5.1, p.200 Problem 5.2\n[21] W. Greiner, Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction Forth Edition (Springer, 2001) (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-56826-8) p.217 The Hydrogen Atom, p.236 9.5 Spectrum of a Diatomic Molecule\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.CoulombTwoBodies.CoulombTwoBody"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.potential-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"potential(model::CoulombTwoBody, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = - fracz_1 z_2 e^24pivarepsilon_0 r \n  = - frace^24pivarepsilon_0 a_0 fracz_1 z_2ra_0\n  = - fracz_1 z_2ra_0 E_mathrmh\nendaligned\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.energy-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"energy(model::CoulombTwoBody; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n\n= -frac(z_1 z_2)^22n^2 fracmum_mathrme E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of particle 1 and particle 2, E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. About atomic units, see section 3.9.2 of the IUPAC GreenBook. In other units, E_mathrmh = 27211386245988(53)mathrmeV from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any, Any, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"wavefunction(model::CoulombTwoBody, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=1, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"radial_function(model::CoulombTwoBody, r; n=1, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = -sqrtfrac(n-l-1)2n(n+l) left(frac2Zn a_muright)^3 left(frac2Zrn a_muright)^l exp left(-fracZrn a_muright) L_n+l^2l+1 left(frac2Zrn a_muright)\n\nwhere frac1mu = frac1m_1+frac1m_2, a_mu = a_0 fracm_mathrmemu, Z = - z_1 z_2, the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right), and the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x). Note that replace 2n(n+l) with 2n(n+l)^3 if the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right). Note that replace L_n+l^2l+1(x) with - L_n-l-1^2l+1(x) if the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = (-1)^k fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n+k(x), which we call the generalized Laguerre polynomials. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::CoulombTwoBody, x; n=0, k=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), not the generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\nL_n^k(x)\n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x) \n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right) \n  = sum_m=0^n-k (-1)^m+k fracnm(m+k)(n-m-k) x^m \n  = (-1)^k L_n-k^(k)(x)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x)=frac1nmathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right).\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^0(x) = 1 \n  L_1^0(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^1(x) = 1 \n  L_2^0(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^1(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^2(x) = 1 \n  L_3^0(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^1(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^2(x) = 3 - x \n  L_3^3(x) = 1 \n  L_4^0(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 512 x^4 \n  L_4^1(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^2(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_4^3(x) = 4 - x \n  L_4^4(x) = 1 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::CoulombTwoBody, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"CoulombTwoBody/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}-CoulombTwoBody","page":"Coulomb 2-Body System","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::CoulombTwoBody, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#developer-guide","page":"Developer Guide","text":"If you are planning significant changes, please open an issue first. The ColPrac guidelines are recommended. For Julia package development basics, see:\n\nHow to develop a Julia package\nPkg: Creating packages","title":"Developer Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Local-Setup","page":"Developer Guide","text":"This procedure is required only once. Install Git and Julia on your local machine before starting.\n\nFork the repository on GitHub.\nClone the forked repository. Replace xxxxxx with your GitHub username.\ngit clone https://github.com/xxxxxx/Antique.jl.git\ncd Antique.jl\nInstall development tools: Revise.jl and Runic.jl.\njulia --startup-file=no -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.add(\"Revise\")'\njulia --project=@runic --startup-file=no -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.add(Pkg.PackageSpec(name = \"Runic\", version = \"1\"))'","title":"Local Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Development-Flow","page":"Developer Guide","text":"This is the typical workflow for making changes.\n\nCreate a branch for your changes. Replace xxx with the issue number (e.g. issue/123).\ngit switch -c issue/xxx\nStart an interactive session with Revise.jl.\njulia --startup-file=no -i -e 'using Revise; import Pkg; Pkg.activate(\".\"); using Antique'\nChange the source code. When making new functions or updating docstrings, refer to Documenter: Adding docstrings.\nIf you need a new dependency, use julia --project=. --startup-file=no -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.add(\"SomePackage\"); Pkg.resolve(); Pkg.instantiate()'. Replace SomePackage with the actual package name.\nFormat the source code with Runic.jl.\njulia --project=@runic --startup-file=no -e 'using Runic; exit(Runic.main(ARGS))' -- --inplace .\njulia --project=@runic --startup-file=no -e 'using Runic; exit(Runic.main(ARGS))' -- --check .\nRun the tests. It will take a few minutes.\njulia --project=. --startup-file=no -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\nBuild the documentation locally. HTML files (docs/build/*.html) will be generated. Please check them with Chrome or any other web browsers.\njulia --project=docs --startup-file=no -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(PackageSpec(path=pwd())); Pkg.instantiate();'\njulia --project=docs --startup-file=no -e 'include(\"docs/make.jl\")'\nCommit and Push the codes (after steps 5–7 succeed).\ngit add \"path/to/changed/file\"\ngit commit -m \"commit message\"\ngit push origin issue/xxx\nSubmit a pull request on GitHub.","title":"Development Flow"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Adding-New-Models","page":"Developer Guide","text":"This is the guideline for adding new models. Adding a new model may take from a few days to a few weeks due to reference search, test implementation, and writing documentation.\n\nFirst, please submit a new issue or comment here. I will assign you to the issue. We need to find orthodox references (textbooks or papers, not Wikipedia) for the analytical solutions (eigenvalues and eigenfunctions) before the development. This will take more time than you think.\nFinish Local Setup and follow Development Flow.\nPlease create 3 files:\nsrc/ModelName.jl — Write the source code and docstrings in this file. The most helpful examples are the harmonic oscillator for one-dimensional systems and the hydrogen atom for three-dimensional systems. We recommend that you copy these files. First create a structure struct ModelName with the same name as the model name (The best way is Find & Replace). Because the function names conflict, you must always give the struct ModelName as the first argument to potential, energy, wavefunction and other functions. Multi-dispatch avoids conflicts. We recommend using Revise.jl while coding (Development Flow).\ntest/ModelName.jl — Write test code in this file. At a minimum, please check the normalization and the orthogonality of the eigenfunctions using QuadGK.jl. Please also do tests for the eigenvalues (for example, calculate the expectation values of the Hamiltonian (energy) using the eigenfunctions and check that these values match the eigenvalues).\ndocs/src/ModelName.md — Write documentation in this file. Include at least the definition of the Hamiltonian and the analytical solutions (eigenvalues and eigenfunctions). Call a docstring in the source code (src/ModelName.jl).\nPlease rewrite 3 files:\nsrc/Antique.jl — Add export ModelName, add :ModelName to models = [...], add include(\"./ModelName.jl\"), and add using .ModelNames: ModelName.\ndocs/make.jl — Add the new model into pages=[...] in this file.\ndocs/src/index.md — Add the new model to the list of supported models.\nAs described in Development Flow, please complete the following steps: run the tests, build and review the documentation, commit, push, and submit a pull request.","title":"Adding New Models"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Versioning-and-Registering-(for-Maintainers)","page":"Developer Guide","text":"This project follows Semantic Versioning. When bumping the version, update the version number in:\n\nProject.toml\nindex.md\n\nTo register this package in the General registry, install Registrator and use it via the GitHub App.","title":"Versioning and Registering (for Maintainers)"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Harmonic-Oscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"The harmonic oscillator is the most frequently used model in quantum physics.","title":"Harmonic Oscillator"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Definitions","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Harmonic-Oscillator-Potential","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Harmonic-Oscillator-Eigenvalues","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Harmonic-Oscillator-Eigenfunctions","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Hermite-Polynomials","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"","title":"Hermite Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by HarmonicOscillator and several parameters k, m and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nHO = HarmonicOscillator(k=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nHO.k\nHO.m\nHO.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(HO, n=0)\nenergy(HO, n=1)\n\nPotential energy curve:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x)$\")\nlines!(ax, -5..5, x -> potential(HO, x))\nf\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# setting\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(x)$\")\n\n# plot\nw0 = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(HO, x, n=0))\nw1 = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(HO, x, n=1))\nw2 = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(HO, x, n=2))\nw3 = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(HO, x, n=3))\nw4 = lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> wavefunction(HO, x, n=4))\n\n# legend\naxislegend(ax, [w0, w1, w2, w3, w4], [L\"n=0\", L\"n=1\", L\"n=2\", L\"n=3\", L\"n=4\"], position=:lb)\n\nf\n\nPotential energy curve, Energy levels, Wave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$V(x),~E_n,~\\psi_n(x) \\times 5 + E_n$\", aspect=1, limits=(-5,5,0,5.2))\n# hidespines!(ax)\n# hidedecorations!(ax)\n\nfor n in 0:4\n  # classical turning point\n  xE = sqrt(2*HO.k*energy(HO, n=n))\n  # energy\n  lines!(ax, [-xE,xE], fill(energy(HO,n=n),2), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n  hlines!(ax, energy(HO, n=n), color=:black, linewidth=1, linestyle=:dash)\n  # wave function\n  lines!(ax, -5..5, x -> energy(HO,n=n) + 0.5*wavefunction(HO,x,n=n), linewidth=2)\nend\n\n#potential\nlines!(ax, -5..5, x -> potential(HO, x), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/HarmonicOscillator.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Testing","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/HarmonicOscillator.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Antique.HarmonicOscillators.HarmonicOscillator-HarmonicOscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 + frac12 k x^2\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nHO = HarmonicOscillator(k=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nk is the force constant, m is the mass of the particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\nMain:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF) 18.5.18\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference cpprefjp, laguerre_polynomial\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) p.48, 2.3.2 Analytic Method\n\nSupplemental:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF)                                                    18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.13, 18.5.18\n[5] L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, Quantum Mechanics (Pergamon Press, 1965)                                  p.595 (a.4), (a.6)\n[6] L. I. Schiff, Quantum Mechanics (McGraw-Hill Book Company, 1968)                                        p.71 (13.12)\n[7] A. Messiah, Quanfum Mechanics (Dover Publications, 1999)                                                p.491 (B.59)\n[8] W. Greiner, Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction Third Edition (Springer, 1994)                           p.152 (7.22)\n[9] D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics (Prentice Hall, 1995)                                p.41 Table 2.1, p.43 (2.70)\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach (University Science Books, 1997) p.170 Table 5.2\n[11] P. W. Atkins, J. De Paula, Atkins' Physical Chemistry, 8th edition (W. H. Freeman, 2008)                p.293 Table 9.1\n[12] J. J. Sakurai, J. Napolitano, Modern Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2021) p.524 (B.29)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.HarmonicOscillators.HarmonicOscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Antique.potential-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}-HarmonicOscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"potential(model::HarmonicOscillator, x)\n\nV(x)\n= frac12 k x^2\n= frac12 m omega^2 x^2\n= frac12 hbar omega xi^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm is the angular frequency and xi = sqrtfracmomegahbarx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Antique.energy-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator}-HarmonicOscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"energy(model::HarmonicOscillator; n::Integer=0)\n\nE_n = hbar omega left( n + frac12 right)\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm is the angular frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}-HarmonicOscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"wavefunction(model::HarmonicOscillator, x; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(x) = A_n H_n(xi) expleft( -fracxi^22 right)\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm, xi = sqrtfracmomegahbarx, A_n = sqrtfrac1n 2^n sqrtfracmomegapihbar, H_n(x) = (-1)^n mathrme^x^2 fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx^n mathrme^-x^2 are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"HarmonicOscillator/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}-HarmonicOscillator","page":"Harmonic Oscillator","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::HarmonicOscillator, x; n=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  H_n(x)\n  = (-1)^n mathrme^x^2 fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx^n mathrme^-x^2 \n  = n sum_m=0^lfloor n2 rfloor frac(-1)^mm (n-2m)(2 x)^n-2m\nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  H_0(x)  = 1 \n  H_1(x)  = 2 x \n  H_2(x)  = -2 + 4 x^2 \n  H_3(x)  = -12 x + 8 x^3 \n  H_4(x)  = 12 - 48 x^2 + 16 x^4 \n  H_5(x)  = 120 x - 160 x^3 + 32 x^5 \n  H_6(x)  = -120 + 720 x^2 - 480 x^4 + 64 x^6 \n  H_7(x)  = -1680 x + 3360 x^3 - 1344 x^5 + 128 x^7 \n  H_8(x)  = 1680 - 13440 x^2 + 13440 x^4 - 3584 x^6 + 256 x^8 \n  H_9(x)  = 30240 x - 80640 x^3 + 48384 x^5 - 9216 x^7 + 512 x^9 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#API-reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.energy","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model; numbers..., parameters...)\n\nReturn an eigenvalue for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.hermite_polynomial","page":"API Reference","text":"hermite_polynomial(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate a Hermite polynomial for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.hermite_polynomial"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate a Laguerre polynomial for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate a Rodrigues-form polynomial/function for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.n_max","page":"API Reference","text":"n_max(model)\n\nReturn the maximum quantum number for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.n_max"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.potential","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model, args...)\n\nReturn the potential for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.radial_function","page":"API Reference","text":"radial_function(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a radial function for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.spherical_harmonic","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_harmonic(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate a spherical harmonic for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model, args...; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a wavefunction for a given model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.DeltaPotentials.DeltaPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 - alpha delta(x)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nDP = DeltaPotential(alpha=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nalpha is the potential strength, m is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018), (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.63, 2.5.2 The Delta-Function Well\n[2] UCSD Physics 130, Quantum Physics, (https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node154.html)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.DeltaPotentials.DeltaPotential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{DeltaPotential}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::DeltaPotential)\n\nE = - fracmalpha^22hbar^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{DeltaPotential, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::DeltaPotential, x)\n\nV(x) = -alpha delta(x)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{DeltaPotential, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::DeltaPotential, x)\n\npsi(x) = fracsqrtmalphahbar mathrme^-malpha xhbar^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.InfinitePotentialWells.InfinitePotentialWell","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 + V(x)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nIPW = InfinitePotentialWell(L=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nL is the length of the box, m is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.31, 2.2 THE INFINITE SQUARE WELL\n\nProofs\n\nEigen Functions & Eigen Values\nNormalization\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.InfinitePotentialWells.InfinitePotentialWell"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::InfinitePotentialWell; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n = frachbar^2 n^2 pi^22 m L^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::InfinitePotentialWell; x)\n\nV(x) =\nleft\n  beginarrayll\n  0       0 leq x leq L \n  infty  x lt 0 L lt x\n  endarray\nright\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::InfinitePotentialWell, x; n::Integer=1)\n\npsi_n(x) = sqrtfrac2L sin fracnpi xL\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.HarmonicOscillators.HarmonicOscillator","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2 + frac12 k x^2\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nHO = HarmonicOscillator(k=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nk is the force constant, m is the mass of the particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\nMain:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF) 18.5.18\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference cpprefjp, laguerre_polynomial\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) p.48, 2.3.2 Analytic Method\n\nSupplemental:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF)                                                    18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.13, 18.5.18\n[5] L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, Quantum Mechanics (Pergamon Press, 1965)                                  p.595 (a.4), (a.6)\n[6] L. I. Schiff, Quantum Mechanics (McGraw-Hill Book Company, 1968)                                        p.71 (13.12)\n[7] A. Messiah, Quanfum Mechanics (Dover Publications, 1999)                                                p.491 (B.59)\n[8] W. Greiner, Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction Third Edition (Springer, 1994)                           p.152 (7.22)\n[9] D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics (Prentice Hall, 1995)                                p.41 Table 2.1, p.43 (2.70)\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach (University Science Books, 1997) p.170 Table 5.2\n[11] P. W. Atkins, J. De Paula, Atkins' Physical Chemistry, 8th edition (W. H. Freeman, 2008)                p.293 Table 9.1\n[12] J. J. Sakurai, J. Napolitano, Modern Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2021) p.524 (B.29)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.HarmonicOscillators.HarmonicOscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::HarmonicOscillator; n::Integer=0)\n\nE_n = hbar omega left( n + frac12 right)\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm is the angular frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::HarmonicOscillator, x; n=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  H_n(x)\n  = (-1)^n mathrme^x^2 fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx^n mathrme^-x^2 \n  = n sum_m=0^lfloor n2 rfloor frac(-1)^mm (n-2m)(2 x)^n-2m\nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  H_0(x)  = 1 \n  H_1(x)  = 2 x \n  H_2(x)  = -2 + 4 x^2 \n  H_3(x)  = -12 x + 8 x^3 \n  H_4(x)  = 12 - 48 x^2 + 16 x^4 \n  H_5(x)  = 120 x - 160 x^3 + 32 x^5 \n  H_6(x)  = -120 + 720 x^2 - 480 x^4 + 64 x^6 \n  H_7(x)  = -1680 x + 3360 x^3 - 1344 x^5 + 128 x^7 \n  H_8(x)  = 1680 - 13440 x^2 + 13440 x^4 - 3584 x^6 + 256 x^8 \n  H_9(x)  = 30240 x - 80640 x^3 + 48384 x^5 - 9216 x^7 + 512 x^9 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::HarmonicOscillator, x)\n\nV(x)\n= frac12 k x^2\n= frac12 m omega^2 x^2\n= frac12 hbar omega xi^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm is the angular frequency and xi = sqrtfracmomegahbarx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{HarmonicOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::HarmonicOscillator, x; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(x) = A_n H_n(xi) expleft( -fracxi^22 right)\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkm, xi = sqrtfracmomegahbarx, A_n = sqrtfrac1n 2^n sqrtfracmomegapihbar, H_n(x) = (-1)^n mathrme^x^2 fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx^n mathrme^-x^2 are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.MorsePotentials.MorsePotential","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(r) = E psi(r)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu fracmathrmd^2mathrmdr ^2 + D_mathrme left( mathrme^-2a(r-r_e) - 2mathrme^-a(r-r_e) right)\n\nwhere a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ is defined. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nMP = MorsePotential(r_e=2.0, D_e=0.1, k=0.1, mu=918.1, hbar=1.0)\n\nr_mathrme is the equilibrium bond distance, D_mathrme is the the well depth , k is the force constant, mu is the reduced mass and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[13] P. M. Morse, Phys. Rev., 34, 57 (1929), (https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.34.57)\n[14] J. P. Dahl, M. Springborg, J. Chem. Phys., 88, 4535 (1988). (62), (63) (https://doi.org/10.1063/1.453761)\n[15] W. K. Shao, Y. He, J. Pan, J. Nonlinear Sci. Appl., 9, 5, 3388 (2016). (1.6) (http://dx.doi.org/10.22436/jnsa.009.05.124)\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF) 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.12, 18.5.17_5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.MorsePotentials.MorsePotential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{MorsePotential}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::MorsePotential; n::Integer=0, nocheck=false)\n\nE_n = - D_mathrme + hbar omega left( n + frac12 right) - chi hbar omega left( n + frac12 right)^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkµ and chi = frachbaromega4D_mathrme are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::MorsePotential, x; n=0, α=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), not the associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_n^(alpha)(x)\n  = fracx^-alphae^xn fracd^ndx^nleft(x^n+alphae^-xright) \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k left(beginarrayl n+alpha  n-k endarrayright) fracx^kk  \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k fracGamma(alpha+n+1)Gamma(alpha+k+1)Gamma(n-k+1) fracx^kk \nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^(0)(x) = 1 \n  L_1^(0)(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^(1)(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^(0)(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(1)(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(2)(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^(0)(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(1)(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(2)(x) = 10 - 10 x + 52 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(3)(x) = 20 - 15 x + 3 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^(0)(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(1)(x) = 5 - 10 x + 5 x^2 - 56 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(2)(x) = 15 - 20 x + 152 x^2 - 1 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(3)(x) = 35 - 35 x + 212 x^2 - 76 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(4)(x) = 70 - 56 x + 14 x^2 - 43 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.n_max-Tuple{MorsePotential}","page":"API Reference","text":"n_max(model::MorsePotential)\n\nnote: Note\nNote that the number of bound states is equal to the maximum quantum number n_max, since we count the ground state from n=1 in this model.\n\nn_mathrmmax = leftlfloor frac2 D_e - omegaomega rightrfloor\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkµ is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.n_max"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::MorsePotential, r)\n\nV(r) = D_mathrme left( mathrme^-2a(r-r_e) - 2mathrme^-a(r-r_e) right)\n\nwhere a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ is defined. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::MorsePotential, r; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(r) = N_n z^lambda-n-12 mathrme^-z2 L_n^(2lambda-2n-1)(xi)\n\nN_n = sqrtfracn(2lambda-2n-1)aGamma(2lambda-n), lambda = fracsqrt2mu D_mathrmeahbar, a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ, L_n^(alpha)(x) = fracx^-alpha mathrme^xn  fracmathrmd^nmathrmd x^nleft(mathrme^-x x^n+alpharight), xi = 2lambdamathrme^-a(r-r_e) are defined. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.PoschlTellers.PoschlTeller","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(x) = E psi(x)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH =\n  - frachbar^22 m fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx ^2\n  - frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(xx_0)\n\nAfter introducing the dimensionless variables\n\n  x^ast equiv xx_0qquad E^ast equiv frachbar^2m x_0^2 E\n\nthe Schrödinger equation reduces to\n\n  hatH^ast psi(x^ast) = E^ast psi(x^ast)\n\nwith\n\n  hatH^ast = - frac12 fracmathrmd^2mathrmdx^ast^2 - fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(x^ast)\n\nParameters are specified within the following struct:\n\nPT = PoschlTeller(lambda=1, m=1.0, hbar=1.0, x_0=1.0)\n\nlambda determines the potential strength. Currently only integer values for lambda are supported.\n\nReferences\n\n[16] G. Pöschl, E. Teller, Zeitschrift für Physik, 83 (3–4), 143 (1933), (https://doi.org/10.1007%2FBF01331132). More general definitions are given as (2a), (2b) or (11)\n[17] S. Flügge, Practical Quantum Mechanics (Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 1999), (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61995-3) p.94 Problem 39. Potential hole of modified Poschl-Teller type\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.PoschlTellers.PoschlTeller"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{PoschlTeller}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::PoschlTeller; n::Integer=0, nocheck=false)\n\nE_n = -frachbar^2m x_0^2fracmu^22\n\nwhere mu = mu(n) = n_mathrmmax-n+1, and n_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::PoschlTeller, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nAssociated Legendre polynomials are the associated Legendre functions for integer indices. Here we use the same notation of the associated Legendre functions as in the model HydrogenAtom.\n\nbeginaligned\nP_n^m(x)\n= left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n= left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n= frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.n_max-Tuple{PoschlTeller}","page":"API Reference","text":"n_max(model::PoschlTeller)\n\nnote: Note\nNote that the number of bound states n_max + 1 is not equal to the maximum quantum number n_max, since we count the ground state from n=0 in this model.\n\nn_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.n_max"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::PoschlTeller, x)\n\nbeginaligned\nV(x)\n= -frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 mathrmsech^2(xx_0) \n= -frachbar^2m x_0^2 fraclambda(lambda+1)2 frac1mathrmcosh^2(xx_0) \nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{PoschlTeller, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::PoschlTeller, x; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(x) = frac(-1)^μsqrtx_0 P_lambda^mu(mathrmtanh(xx_0)) sqrtmufracGamma(lambda-mu+1)Gamma(lambda+mu+1)\n\nwhere mu = mu(n) = n_mathrmmax-n+1, and n_mathrmmax = leftlfloor lambda rightrfloor - 1 and P_lambda^mu are the associated Legendre functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.RigidRotors.RigidRotor","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(thetavarphi) = E psi(thetavarphi)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\nbeginaligned\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + V(r) \n          = - frachbar^22I left frac1sintheta fracpartialpartialtheta left(sintheta fracpartialpartialthetaright) + frac1sin^2theta fracpartial^2partialphi^2  right \n          = fracL^22I\nendaligned\n\nwhere I=mu R^2 is the moment of intertia, mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of two particles, R is the distance between the two particles, and L^2 is the angular momentum operator. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nRR = RigidRotor(m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, R=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nm₁ and m₂ are mass of two particles, R is the distance, and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical chemistry : a molecular approach (University Science Books, 1997) (https://mitpress.mit.edu/9781940380216/physical-chemistry/) p.173, 5.8 The Energy Levels of a Rigid Rotator Are E = hbar^2 J(J+1)  2I\n[18] Chemistry Libre Texts, (https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/PhysicalandTheoreticalChemistryTextbookMaps/PhysicalChemistry(LibreTexts)/05%3ATheHarmonicOscillatorandtheRigidRotor/5.08%3ATheEnergyLevelsofaRigid_Rotor)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.RigidRotors.RigidRotor"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{RigidRotor}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::RigidRotor; l::Integer=0)\n\nE_l\n= frachbar^22Il(l+1)\n\nwhere I=mu R^2 is the moment of inertia, R is the distance, and mu is the reduced mass of the two particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::RigidRotor, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::RigidRotor, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = 0\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::RigidRotor, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::RigidRotor, θ, φ; l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_lm(thetavarphi) = Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe wave functions are the spherical harmonics. The domain is 0leq theta lt pi and 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.InfinitePotentialWell3Ds.InfinitePotentialWell3D","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(xyz) = E psi(xyz)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m left(fracpartial^2partial x ^2 + fracpartial^2partial y ^2 + fracpartial^2partial z ^2right) + V(xyz)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nIPW3D = InfinitePotentialWell3D(L=[1.0,1.0,1.0], m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nL is a vector of the lengths of the box in x,y,z-direction, m is the mass of the particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical chemistry : a molecular approach (University Science Books, 1997), (https://mitpress.mit.edu/9781940380216/physical-chemistry/) p.90, 3-9. The Problem of a Particle in a Three-Dimensional Box Is a Simple Extension of the One-Dimensional Case\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.InfinitePotentialWell3Ds.InfinitePotentialWell3D"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D; n::Vector{Int}=[1,1,1])\n\nE_n_xn_yn_z\n= frachbar^2 n_x^2 pi^22 m L_x^2\n+ frachbar^2 n_y^2 pi^22 m L_y^2\n+ frachbar^2 n_z^2 pi^22 m L_z^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D, x)\n\nV(xyz) =\nleft\n  beginarrayll\n  0       0 leq x leq L_x  mathrmand  0 leq y leq L_y  mathrmand  0 leq z leq L_z \n  infty  mathrmelsewhere\n  endarray\nright\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D, x; n::Vector{Int}=[1,1,1])\n\nThe wave functions can be expressed as products of wave functions in a one-dimensional box.\n\nbeginaligned\n  psi_n_xn_yn_z(xyz)\n  =     psi_n_x(x)\n  times psi_n_y(y)\n  times psi_n_z(z) \n  =     sqrtfrac2L_x sin fracn_x pi xL_x\n  times sqrtfrac2L_y sin fracn_y pi yL_y\n  times sqrtfrac2L_z sin fracn_z pi zL_z\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.SphericalOscillators.SphericalOscillator","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + frac12 k r^2\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nSO = SphericalOscillator(k=1.0, mu=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nk is the force constant, μ is the mass of particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[17] S. Flügge, Practical Quantum Mechanics (Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 1999), (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61995-3) [p.166, Problem 65. Spherical oscillator].\n[19] UCSD Physics 130, Quantum Physics, (https://quantummechanics.ucsd.edu/ph130a/130_notes/node244.html)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.SphericalOscillators.SphericalOscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{SphericalOscillator}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::SphericalOscillator; n::Integer=0, l::Integer=0)\n\nE_nl\n= left(2n + l + frac32right)hbar omega\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkmu.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::SphericalOscillator, x; n=0, α=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), not the associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_n^(alpha)(x)\n  = fracx^-alphae^xn fracd^ndx^nleft(x^n+alphae^-xright) \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k left(beginarrayl n+alpha  n-k endarrayright) fracx^kk  \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k fracGamma(alpha+n+1)Gamma(alpha+k+1)Gamma(n-k+1) fracx^kk \nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^(0)(x) = 1 \n  L_1^(0)(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^(1)(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^(0)(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(1)(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(2)(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^(0)(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(1)(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(2)(x) = 10 - 10 x + 52 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(3)(x) = 20 - 15 x + 3 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^(0)(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(1)(x) = 5 - 10 x + 5 x^2 - 56 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(2)(x) = 15 - 20 x + 152 x^2 - 1 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(3)(x) = 35 - 35 x + 212 x^2 - 76 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(4)(x) = 70 - 56 x + 14 x^2 - 43 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::SphericalOscillator, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::SphericalOscillator, r)\n\nV(r)\n= frac12 k r^2\n= frac12 mu omega^2 r^2\n= frac12 hbar omega xi^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkmu is the angular frequency and xi = sqrtfracmuomegahbarr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"radial_function(model::SphericalOscillator, r; n=0, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = sqrt fracgamma^322sqrtpi sqrtfrac2^n+l+3 n(2n+2l+1) xi^l expleft(-xi^22right)L_n^(l+frac12) left(xi^2right)\n\nwhere gamma = muomegahbar and xi = sqrtgammar = sqrtmuomegahbarr are defined. The generalized Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^(alpha)(x) = fracx^-alpha mathrme^xn  fracmathrmd^nmathrmd x^nleft(mathrme^-x x^n+alpharight). The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::SphericalOscillator, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{SphericalOscillator, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::SphericalOscillator, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=0, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.HydrogenAtoms.HydrogenAtom","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 - fracZra_0 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_mathrme+frac1m_mathrmpright)^-1 is the reduced mass of electron mathrme and proton mathrmp. mu = m_mathrme holds in the limit m_mathrmprightarrowinfty. The potential includes only Coulomb interaction and it does not include fine or hyperfine interactions in this model. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nHA = HydrogenAtom(Z=1, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nZ is the atomic number, m_mathrme is the electron mass, a_0 is the Bohr radius, E_mathrmh is the Hartree energy and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\nMain:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference cpprefjp, assoc_legendre, assoc_laguerre\n[20] A. Messiah, Quantum Mechanics VOLUME Ⅰ (North-Holland Publishing Company, 1961), p.412 (XI.3), p.419 (XI.18) (XI.18a) (XI.18b), p.483 (B.12), p.493 (B.71) (B.72), p.494 (B.81), p495 (B.93)\n\nSupplemental:\n\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference legendre, assoc_legendre, laguerre_polynomial, assoc_laguerre\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17, 18.5.12\n[5] L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, Quantum Mechanics (Pergamon Press, 1965), p.598 (c.1), p.598 (c.4), p.603 (d.13)\n[6] L. I. Schiff, Quantum Mechanics (McGraw-Hill Book Company, 1968), p.79 (14.12), p.93 (16.19)\n[7] A. Messiah, Quanfum Mechanics (Dover Publications, 1999), p.493 (B.72), p.494 Table, p.493 (B.72), p.483 (B.12), p.483 (B.１２)\n[9] D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics (Prentice Hall, 1995), p.126 (4.28), p.96 Table3.1, p.126 (4.27), p.139 (4.88), p.140 Table4.4, p.139 (4.87), p.140 Table4.5\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach (University Science Books, 1997), p.195 Table6.1, p.196 (6.26), p.196 Table6.2, p.207 Table6.4\n[11] P. W. Atkins, J. De Paula, Atkins' Physical Chemistry, 8th edition (W. H. Freeman, 2008), p.234\n[12] J. J. Sakurai, J. Napolitano, Modern Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2021), p.245 Problem 3.30.b \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.HydrogenAtoms.HydrogenAtom"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{HydrogenAtom}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::HydrogenAtom; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n\n= -fracm_mathrme e^4 Z^22n^2(4pivarepsilon_0)^2hbar^2\n= -fracZ^22n^2 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. About atomic units, see section 3.9.2 of the IUPAC GreenBook. In other units, E_mathrmh = 27211386245988(53)mathrmeV from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::HydrogenAtom, x; n=0, k=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), not the generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\nL_n^k(x)\n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x) \n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right) \n  = sum_m=0^n-k (-1)^m+k fracnm(m+k)(n-m-k) x^m \n  = (-1)^k L_n-k^(k)(x)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x)=frac1nmathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right).\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^0(x) = 1 \n  L_1^0(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^1(x) = 1 \n  L_2^0(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^1(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^2(x) = 1 \n  L_3^0(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^1(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^2(x) = 3 - x \n  L_3^3(x) = 1 \n  L_4^0(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 512 x^4 \n  L_4^1(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^2(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_4^3(x) = 4 - x \n  L_4^4(x) = 1 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::HydrogenAtom, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::HydrogenAtom, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = - fracZe^24pivarepsilon_0 r \n  = - frace^24pivarepsilon_0 a_0 fracZra_0\n  = - fracZra_0 E_mathrmh\nendaligned\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"radial_function(model::HydrogenAtom, r; n=1, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = -sqrtfrac(n-l-1)2n(n+l) left(frac2Zn a_0right)^3 left(frac2Zrn a_0right)^l exp left(-fracZrn a_0right) L_n+l^2l+1 left(frac2Zrn a_0right)\n\nwhere the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right), and the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x). Note that replace 2n(n+l) with 2n(n+l)^3 if the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right). Note that replace L_n+l^2l+1(x) with - L_n-l-1^2l+1(x) if the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = (-1)^k fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n+k(x), which we call the generalized Laguerre polynomials. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::HydrogenAtom, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::HydrogenAtom, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=1, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Antique.CoulombTwoBodies.CoulombTwoBody","page":"API Reference","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + fracz_1 z_2ra_0 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of particle 1 and particle 2. The potential includes only Coulomb interaction and it does not include fine or hyperfine interactions in this model. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nCTB = CoulombTwoBody(z_1=-1, z_2=1, m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nz₁ is the charge number of particle 1,  z₂ is the charge number of particle 2,  m₁ is the mass of particle 1,  m₂ is the mass of particle 2, m_mathrme is the electron mass (use the same unit as m₁ and m₂. For example of hydrogen atom, use m_mathrme=91093837139times10^-31mathrmkg, m_1=91093837139times10^-31mathrmkg and m_2=167262192595times10^-27mathrmkg in the IS unit system, use m_mathrme=10m_mathrme, m_1=10m_mathrme and m_2=1836152673426m_mathrme in the atomic unit.), a_0 is the Bohr radius, E_mathrmh is the Hartree energy and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference (C++日本語リファレンス) cpprefjp, assoc_legendre, assoc_laguerre\n[20] A. Messiah, Quantum Mechanics VOLUME Ⅰ (North-Holland Publishing Company, 1961), p.412 I. THE HYDROGEN ATOM\n[1] D. J. Griffiths, D. F. Schroeter, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2018) (https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316995433) p.143 4.2 THE HYDROGEN ATOM, p.200 Problem 5.1, p.200 Problem 5.2\n[21] W. Greiner, Quantum Mechanics: An Introduction Forth Edition (Springer, 2001) (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-56826-8) p.217 The Hydrogen Atom, p.236 9.5 Spectrum of a Diatomic Molecule\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.CoulombTwoBodies.CoulombTwoBody"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.energy-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody}","page":"API Reference","text":"energy(model::CoulombTwoBody; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n\n= -frac(z_1 z_2)^22n^2 fracmum_mathrme E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of particle 1 and particle 2, E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. About atomic units, see section 3.9.2 of the IUPAC GreenBook. In other units, E_mathrmh = 27211386245988(53)mathrmeV from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::CoulombTwoBody, x; n=0, k=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), not the generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\nL_n^k(x)\n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x) \n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right) \n  = sum_m=0^n-k (-1)^m+k fracnm(m+k)(n-m-k) x^m \n  = (-1)^k L_n-k^(k)(x)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x)=frac1nmathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right).\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^0(x) = 1 \n  L_1^0(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^1(x) = 1 \n  L_2^0(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^1(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^2(x) = 1 \n  L_3^0(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^1(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^2(x) = 3 - x \n  L_3^3(x) = 1 \n  L_4^0(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 512 x^4 \n  L_4^1(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^2(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_4^3(x) = 4 - x \n  L_4^4(x) = 1 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::CoulombTwoBody, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.potential-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"potential(model::CoulombTwoBody, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = - fracz_1 z_2 e^24pivarepsilon_0 r \n  = - frace^24pivarepsilon_0 a_0 fracz_1 z_2ra_0\n  = - fracz_1 z_2ra_0 E_mathrmh\nendaligned\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"radial_function(model::CoulombTwoBody, r; n=1, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = -sqrtfrac(n-l-1)2n(n+l) left(frac2Zn a_muright)^3 left(frac2Zrn a_muright)^l exp left(-fracZrn a_muright) L_n+l^2l+1 left(frac2Zrn a_muright)\n\nwhere frac1mu = frac1m_1+frac1m_2, a_mu = a_0 fracm_mathrmemu, Z = - z_1 z_2, the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right), and the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x). Note that replace 2n(n+l) with 2n(n+l)^3 if the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right). Note that replace L_n+l^2l+1(x) with - L_n-l-1^2l+1(x) if the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = (-1)^k fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n+k(x), which we call the generalized Laguerre polynomials. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::CoulombTwoBody, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"API/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{CoulombTwoBody, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"wavefunction(model::CoulombTwoBody, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=1, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Morse-Potential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"The Morse potential is a model for inter-nuclear anharmonic vibration in a diatomic molecule.","title":"Morse Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Definitions","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Potential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Eigenvalues","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Maximum-Quantum-Number","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Maximum Quantum Number"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Generalized-Laguerre-Polynomials","page":"Morse Potential","text":"","title":"Generalized Laguerre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Morse Potential","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction(), potential potential() and n_max() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by MorsePotential and several parameters r_e, D_e, k, mu and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\n# Parameters for H₂⁺\n# https://doi.org/10.1002/slct.202102509\n# https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5047817\n# https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mpsme\nr_e =  1.997193319969992120068298141276\nD_e = - 0.5 - (-0.602634619106539878727562156289)\nk = 2*((-1.1026342144949464615+1/2.00) - (-0.602634619106539878727562156289)) / (2.00 - r_e)^2\nmu = 1/(1/1836.15267343 + 1/1836.15267343)\nhbar = 1.0\n\nusing Antique\nMP = MorsePotential(r_e=r_e, D_e=D_e, k=k, mu=mu, hbar=hbar)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nMP.r_e\nMP.D_e\nMP.k\nMP.mu\nMP.hbar\n\nMaximum quantum number:\n\nn_max(MP)\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(MP, n=0)\nenergy(MP, n=1)\n\nPotential energy curve:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r$\", ylabel=L\"$V(r)$\",  limits=(0.0,20.0,-0.11,0.1))\nlines!(ax, 0.1:0.01:20, r -> potential(MP, r))\nf\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# setting\nfig = Figure()\naxis = Axis(fig[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(r)$\")\n\n# plot\nlines!(axis, 0..5, x -> wavefunction(MP, x, n=0), label=L\"n=0\")\nlines!(axis, 0..5, x -> wavefunction(MP, x, n=1), label=L\"n=1\")\nlines!(axis, 0..5, x -> wavefunction(MP, x, n=2), label=L\"n=2\")\nlines!(axis, 0..5, x -> wavefunction(MP, x, n=3), label=L\"n=3\")\nlines!(axis, 0..5, x -> wavefunction(MP, x, n=4), label=L\"n=4\")\n\n# legend\naxislegend(axis, position=:lb, framevisible=false)\n\nfig\n\nPotential energy curve, Energy levels, Comparison with harmonic oscillator:\n\nusing Antique\n\n# https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mmusme\nm = 206.7682830\nmu = 1 / (1/m + 1/m)\nMP = MorsePotential(mu=mu)\n# @show n_max(MP)\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r$\", ylabel=L\"$V(r),~E_n,~\\psi_n(r) \\times 5 + E_n$\", aspect=1, limits=(0.5,10,-0.105,0.007))\n# hidespines!(ax)\n# hidedecorations!(ax)\n\n@show n_max(MP)\n\nfor n in 0:n_max(MP)\n  # energy\n  EMP = energy(MP, n=n)\n  lines!(ax, 0.1:0.01:15, r -> EMP > potential(MP, r) ? EMP : NaN, color=:black, linewidth=2)\n  hlines!(ax, energy(MP, n=n), color=:black, linewidth=1, linestyle=:dash)\n  # wave function\n  lines!(ax, 0..10, x -> energy(MP,n=n) + 0.0065*wavefunction(MP,x,n=n), linewidth=2)\nend\n\n#potential\nlines!(ax, 0..10, x -> potential(MP, x), color=:black, linewidth=2)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/MorsePotential.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nwhere the potential of harmonic oscillator is defined as V(r) simeq frac12 k (r - r_mathrme)^2 + V_0.","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"MorsePotential/#Testing","page":"Morse Potential","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/MorsePotential.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.MorsePotentials.MorsePotential-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(r) = E psi(r)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu fracmathrmd^2mathrmdr ^2 + D_mathrme left( mathrme^-2a(r-r_e) - 2mathrme^-a(r-r_e) right)\n\nwhere a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ is defined. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nMP = MorsePotential(r_e=2.0, D_e=0.1, k=0.1, mu=918.1, hbar=1.0)\n\nr_mathrme is the equilibrium bond distance, D_mathrme is the the well depth , k is the force constant, mu is the reduced mass and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[13] P. M. Morse, Phys. Rev., 34, 57 (1929), (https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.34.57)\n[14] J. P. Dahl, M. Springborg, J. Chem. Phys., 88, 4535 (1988). (62), (63) (https://doi.org/10.1063/1.453761)\n[15] W. K. Shao, Y. He, J. Pan, J. Nonlinear Sci. Appl., 9, 5, 3388 (2016). (1.6) (http://dx.doi.org/10.22436/jnsa.009.05.124)\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF) 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.12, 18.5.17_5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.MorsePotentials.MorsePotential"},{"category":"method","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.potential-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"potential(model::MorsePotential, r)\n\nV(r) = D_mathrme left( mathrme^-2a(r-r_e) - 2mathrme^-a(r-r_e) right)\n\nwhere a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ is defined. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.energy-Tuple{MorsePotential}-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"energy(model::MorsePotential; n::Integer=0, nocheck=false)\n\nE_n = - D_mathrme + hbar omega left( n + frac12 right) - chi hbar omega left( n + frac12 right)^2\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkµ and chi = frachbaromega4D_mathrme are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.n_max-Tuple{MorsePotential}-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"n_max(model::MorsePotential)\n\nnote: Note\nNote that the number of bound states is equal to the maximum quantum number n_max, since we count the ground state from n=1 in this model.\n\nn_mathrmmax = leftlfloor frac2 D_e - omegaomega rightrfloor\n\nwhere omega = sqrtkµ is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.n_max"},{"category":"method","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"wavefunction(model::MorsePotential, r; n::Integer=0)\n\npsi_n(r) = N_n z^lambda-n-12 mathrme^-z2 L_n^(2lambda-2n-1)(xi)\n\nN_n = sqrtfracn(2lambda-2n-1)aGamma(2lambda-n), lambda = fracsqrt2mu D_mathrmeahbar, a = sqrtfrack2Dₑ, L_n^(alpha)(x) = fracx^-alpha mathrme^xn  fracmathrmd^nmathrmd x^nleft(mathrme^-x x^n+alpharight), xi = 2lambdamathrme^-a(r-r_e) are defined. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"MorsePotential/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{MorsePotential, Any}-MorsePotential","page":"Morse Potential","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::MorsePotential, x; n=0, α=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), not the associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_n^(alpha)(x)\n  = fracx^-alphae^xn fracd^ndx^nleft(x^n+alphae^-xright) \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k left(beginarrayl n+alpha  n-k endarrayright) fracx^kk  \n  = sum_k=0^n(-1)^k fracGamma(alpha+n+1)Gamma(alpha+k+1)Gamma(n-k+1) fracx^kk \nendaligned\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^(0)(x) = 1 \n  L_1^(0)(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^(1)(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^(0)(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(1)(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^(2)(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^(0)(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(1)(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(2)(x) = 10 - 10 x + 52 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^(3)(x) = 20 - 15 x + 3 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^(0)(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(1)(x) = 5 - 10 x + 5 x^2 - 56 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(2)(x) = 15 - 20 x + 152 x^2 - 1 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(3)(x) = 35 - 35 x + 212 x^2 - 76 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  L_4^(4)(x) = 70 - 56 x + 14 x^2 - 43 x^3 + 124 x^4 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Hydrogen-Atom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"The hydrogen atom is the simplest Coulomb 2-body system.","title":"Hydrogen Atom"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Definitions","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Potential","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Eigenvalues","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Radial-Functions","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Radial Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Associated-Laguerre-Polynomials","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Associated Laguerre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Spherical-Harmonics","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Spherical Harmonics"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Associated-Legendre-Polynomials","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"","title":"Associated Legendre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by HydrogenAtom and several parameters Z, E_h, m_e, a_0 and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nH = HydrogenAtom(Z=1, E_h=1.0, a_0=1.0, m_e=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nH.Z\nH.E_h\nH.m_e\nH.a_0\nH.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(H, n=1)\nenergy(H, n=2)\n\nWavelength (n=2rightarrow1, the first line of the Lyman series):\n\nEₕ2nm⁻¹ = 2.1947463136320e-2 # https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/CCValue?hrminv\nprintln(\"ΔE = \", energy(H,n=2) - energy(H,n=1), \" Eₕ\")\nprintln(\"λ  = \", ((energy(H,n=2)-energy(H,n=1))*Eₕ2nm⁻¹)^-1, \" nm\")\n\nHyperfine Splitting:\n\n# E. Tiesinga, et al., Rev. Mod. Phys. 93, 025010 (2021) https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.93.025010\ne  = 1.602176634e-19    # C      https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?e\nh  = 6.62607015e-34     # J Hz-1 https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?h\nc  = 299792458          # m s-1  https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c\na0 = 5.29177210903e-11  # m      https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?bohrrada0\nμ0 = 1.25663706212e-6   # N A-2  https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mu0\nμB = 9.2740100783e-24   # J T-1  https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mub\nμN = 5.0507837461e-27   # J T-1  https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mun\nge = 2.00231930436256   #        https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gem\ngp = 5.5856946893       #        https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?gp\n\n# D. J. Griffiths, Am. J. Phys. 50, 698 (1982) https://doi.org/10.1119/1.12733\nδ = abs(wavefunction(H,0,0,0))^2\nΔE = 2 / 3 * μ0 * μN * μB * gp * ge * δ * a0^(-3)\nprintln(\"1/π    = \", 1/π)\nprintln(\"<δ(r)> = \", δ, \" a₀⁻³\")\nprintln(\"<δ(r)> = \", δ * a0^(-3), \" m⁻³\")\nprintln(\"ΔE = \", ΔE, \" J\")\nprintln(\"ν = ΔE/h = \", ΔE / h * 1e-6, \" MHz\")\nprintln(\"λ = hc/ΔE = \", h*c/ΔE*100, \" cm\")\n\nPotential energy curve:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r~/~a_0$\", ylabel=L\"$V(r)~/~E_\\mathrm{h}$\",  limits=(0.0,15.0,-2.0,0.2))\nlines!(ax, 0.1:0.01:20, r -> potential(H, r))\nf\n\n1S wave function:\n\nimport Antique\nH = Antique.HydrogenAtom(Z=1, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n@show Antique.energy(H)\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(\n    size = (420,300),\n    fontsize = 11,\n    backgroundcolor = :transparent\n)\n\nax = Axis(\n    fig[1,1],\n    xlabel = L\"$r / a_0$\",\n    ylabel = L\"$\\psi(r) / a_0^{-3/2}$\",\n    ylabelsize = 16.5,\n    xlabelsize = 16.5,\n)\n\nlines!(ax, 0..5, r -> exp(-r)/sqrt(π), label=\"exp(-r)/sqrt(π)\")\nlines!(ax, 0..1, r -> (1-r)/sqrt(π), label=\"(1-r)/sqrt(π)\")\nlines!(ax, 0..5, r -> abs(Antique.wavefunction(H,r,0,0)), linestyle=:dash, color=:black, label=\"Antique.jl\")\n\naxislegend(ax, position=:rt, framevisible=false)\n\nfig\n\nRadial functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LaTeXStrings\n\n# setting\nf = Figure()\nax = Axis(f[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r~/~a_0$\", ylabel=L\"$r^2|R_{nl}(r)|^2~/~a_0^{-1}$\", limits=(0,20,0,0.58))\n\n# plot\nws = []\nls = []\nfor n in 1:3\n  for l in 0:n-1\n    w = lines!(\n        ax,\n        0..20,\n        r -> r^2 * Antique.radial_function(H,r,n=n,l=l)^2,\n        linewidth = 2,\n        linestyle = [:solid,:dash,:dot,:dashdot,:dashdotdot][l+1],\n        color = n,\n        colormap = :tab10,\n        colorrange = (1,10)\n    )\n    push!(ws, w)\n    push!(ls, latexstring(\"n=$n, l=$l\"))\n  end\nend\n\n# legend\naxislegend(ax, ws, ls, position=:rt)\n\nf","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Testing","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/HydrogenAtom.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.HydrogenAtoms.HydrogenAtom-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(pmbr) = E psi(pmbr)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 - fracZra_0 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere mu=left(frac1m_mathrme+frac1m_mathrmpright)^-1 is the reduced mass of electron mathrme and proton mathrmp. mu = m_mathrme holds in the limit m_mathrmprightarrowinfty. The potential includes only Coulomb interaction and it does not include fine or hyperfine interactions in this model. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nHA = HydrogenAtom(Z=1, m_e=1.0, a_0=1.0, E_h=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nZ is the atomic number, m_mathrme is the electron mass, a_0 is the Bohr radius, E_mathrmh is the Hartree energy and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\nMain:\n\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference cpprefjp, assoc_legendre, assoc_laguerre\n[20] A. Messiah, Quantum Mechanics VOLUME Ⅰ (North-Holland Publishing Company, 1961), p.412 (XI.3), p.419 (XI.18) (XI.18a) (XI.18b), p.483 (B.12), p.493 (B.71) (B.72), p.494 (B.81), p495 (B.93)\n\nSupplemental:\n\n[4] C++ Japanese Reference legendre, assoc_legendre, laguerre_polynomial, assoc_laguerre\n[3] The Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (DLMF), 18.3 Table1, 18.5 Table1, 18.5.16, 18.5.17, 18.5.12\n[5] L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, Quantum Mechanics (Pergamon Press, 1965), p.598 (c.1), p.598 (c.4), p.603 (d.13)\n[6] L. I. Schiff, Quantum Mechanics (McGraw-Hill Book Company, 1968), p.79 (14.12), p.93 (16.19)\n[7] A. Messiah, Quanfum Mechanics (Dover Publications, 1999), p.493 (B.72), p.494 Table, p.493 (B.72), p.483 (B.12), p.483 (B.１２)\n[9] D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics (Prentice Hall, 1995), p.126 (4.28), p.96 Table3.1, p.126 (4.27), p.139 (4.88), p.140 Table4.4, p.139 (4.87), p.140 Table4.5\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach (University Science Books, 1997), p.195 Table6.1, p.196 (6.26), p.196 Table6.2, p.207 Table6.4\n[11] P. W. Atkins, J. De Paula, Atkins' Physical Chemistry, 8th edition (W. H. Freeman, 2008), p.234\n[12] J. J. Sakurai, J. Napolitano, Modern Quantum Mechanics Third Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2021), p.245 Problem 3.30.b \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.HydrogenAtoms.HydrogenAtom"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.potential-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"potential(model::HydrogenAtom, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = - fracZe^24pivarepsilon_0 r \n  = - frace^24pivarepsilon_0 a_0 fracZra_0\n  = - fracZra_0 E_mathrmh\nendaligned\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.energy-Tuple{HydrogenAtom}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"energy(model::HydrogenAtom; n::Integer=1)\n\nE_n\n= -fracm_mathrme e^4 Z^22n^2(4pivarepsilon_0)^2hbar^2\n= -fracZ^22n^2 E_mathrmh\n\nwhere E_mathrmh = frachbar^2m_mathrmea_0^2 = frace^24pivarepsilon_0a_0 = fracm_mathrmee^4left(4pivarepsilon_0right)^2hbar^2 is the Hartree energy, one of atomic unit. About atomic units, see section 3.9.2 of the IUPAC GreenBook. In other units, E_mathrmh = 27211386245988(53)mathrmeV from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any, Any, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"wavefunction(model::HydrogenAtom, r, θ, φ; n::Integer=1, l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_nlm(pmbr) = R_nl(r) Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe domain is 0leq r lt infty 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.radial_function-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"radial_function(model::HydrogenAtom, r; n=1, l=0)\n\nR_nl(r) = -sqrtfrac(n-l-1)2n(n+l) left(frac2Zn a_0right)^3 left(frac2Zrn a_0right)^l exp left(-fracZrn a_0right) L_n+l^2l+1 left(frac2Zrn a_0right)\n\nwhere the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right), and the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x). Note that replace 2n(n+l) with 2n(n+l)^3 if the Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x) = mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right). Note that replace L_n+l^2l+1(x) with - L_n-l-1^2l+1(x) if the associated Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n^k(x) = (-1)^k fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n+k(x), which we call the generalized Laguerre polynomials. The domain is 0leq r lt infty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.radial_function"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.laguerre_polynomial-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"laguerre_polynomial(model::HydrogenAtom, x; n=0, k=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe associated Laguerre polynomials L_n^k(x), not the generalized Laguerre polynomials L_n^(alpha)(x), are used in this model.\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\nL_n^k(x)\n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k L_n(x) \n  = fracmathrmd^kmathrmdx^k frac1n mathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right) \n  = sum_m=0^n-k (-1)^m+k fracnm(m+k)(n-m-k) x^m \n  = (-1)^k L_n-k^(k)(x)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Laguerre polynomials are defined as L_n(x)=frac1nmathrme^x fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left( mathrme^-x x^n right).\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  L_0^0(x) = 1 \n  L_1^0(x) = 1 - x \n  L_1^1(x) = 1 \n  L_2^0(x) = 1 - 2 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_2^1(x) = 2 - x \n  L_2^2(x) = 1 \n  L_3^0(x) = 1 - 3 x + 32 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_3^1(x) = 3 - 3 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_3^2(x) = 3 - x \n  L_3^3(x) = 1 \n  L_4^0(x) = 1 - 4 x + 3 x^2 - 23 x^3 + 512 x^4 \n  L_4^1(x) = 4 - 6 x + 2 x^2 - 16 x^3 \n  L_4^2(x) = 6 - 4 x + 12 x^2 \n  L_4^3(x) = 4 - x \n  L_4^4(x) = 1 \n  vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.laguerre_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::HydrogenAtom, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"HydrogenAtom/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{HydrogenAtom, Any}-HydrogenAtom","page":"Hydrogen Atom","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::HydrogenAtom, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Infinite-Potential-Well-3D-(Particle-in-a-3D-Box)","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"The three-dimensional infinite potential well (particle in a 3D-box) is one of the simplest models for a three-dimensional quantum mechanical system.","title":"Infinite Potential Well 3D (Particle in a 3D-Box)"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Definitions","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Potential","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Eigenvalues","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by InfinitePotentialWell3D and several parameters Lx, Ly, Lz, m and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nIPW3D = InfinitePotentialWell3D(L=[1.0,1.0,1.0], m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nIPW3D.L\nIPW3D.m\nIPW3D.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[1,1,1])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[2,1,1])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[1,2,1])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[1,1,2])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[2,2,1])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[2,1,2])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[1,2,2])\nenergy(IPW3D, n=[2,2,2])\n\nWave functions:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# settings\nfig = Figure()\naxis = Axis(fig[1,1], xlabel=L\"$x$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(x,0.5,0.5)$\")\n\n# plot\nlines!(axis, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW3D, [x,0.5,0.5], n=[1,1,1]), label=L\"n=[1,1,1]\")\nlines!(axis, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW3D, [x,0.5,0.5], n=[2,1,1]), label=L\"n=[2,1,1]\")\nlines!(axis, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW3D, [x,0.5,0.5], n=[1,2,1]), label=L\"n=[1,2,1]\")\nlines!(axis, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW3D, [x,0.5,0.5], n=[3,1,1]), label=L\"n=[3,1,1]\")\nlines!(axis, 0..1, x -> wavefunction(IPW3D, [x,0.5,0.5], n=[4,1,1]), label=L\"n=[4,1,1]\")\n\n# legend\naxislegend(axis, position=:lb, framevisible=false)\n\nfig","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Testing","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/InfinitePotentialWell3D.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Antique.InfinitePotentialWell3Ds.InfinitePotentialWell3D-InfinitePotentialWell3D","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(xyz) = E psi(xyz)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\n  hatH = - frachbar^22m left(fracpartial^2partial x ^2 + fracpartial^2partial y ^2 + fracpartial^2partial z ^2right) + V(xyz)\n\nParameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nIPW3D = InfinitePotentialWell3D(L=[1.0,1.0,1.0], m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nL is a vector of the lengths of the box in x,y,z-direction, m is the mass of the particle and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical chemistry : a molecular approach (University Science Books, 1997), (https://mitpress.mit.edu/9781940380216/physical-chemistry/) p.90, 3-9. The Problem of a Particle in a Three-Dimensional Box Is a Simple Extension of the One-Dimensional Case\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.InfinitePotentialWell3Ds.InfinitePotentialWell3D"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Antique.potential-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D, Any}-InfinitePotentialWell3D","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"potential(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D, x)\n\nV(xyz) =\nleft\n  beginarrayll\n  0       0 leq x leq L_x  mathrmand  0 leq y leq L_y  mathrmand  0 leq z leq L_z \n  infty  mathrmelsewhere\n  endarray\nright\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Antique.energy-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D}-InfinitePotentialWell3D","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"energy(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D; n::Vector{Int}=[1,1,1])\n\nE_n_xn_yn_z\n= frachbar^2 n_x^2 pi^22 m L_x^2\n+ frachbar^2 n_y^2 pi^22 m L_y^2\n+ frachbar^2 n_z^2 pi^22 m L_z^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"InfinitePotentialWell3D/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{InfinitePotentialWell3D, Any}-InfinitePotentialWell3D","page":"Infinite Potential Well 3D","text":"wavefunction(model::InfinitePotentialWell3D, x; n::Vector{Int}=[1,1,1])\n\nThe wave functions can be expressed as products of wave functions in a one-dimensional box.\n\nbeginaligned\n  psi_n_xn_yn_z(xyz)\n  =     psi_n_x(x)\n  times psi_n_y(y)\n  times psi_n_z(z) \n  =     sqrtfrac2L_x sin fracn_x pi xL_x\n  times sqrtfrac2L_y sin fracn_y pi yL_y\n  times sqrtfrac2L_z sin fracn_z pi zL_z\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Rigid-Rotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"The linear rigid rotor model can be used in quantum mechanics to predict the rotational energy of a diatomic molecule. The rotational energy depends on the moment of inertia for the system, I.","title":"Rigid Rotor"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Definitions","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Potential","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Eigenvalues","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Eigenfunctions","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Eigenfunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Spherical-Harmonics","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Spherical Harmonics"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Associated-Legendre-Polynomials","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"","title":"Associated Legendre Polynomials"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use. The energy energy(), wave function wavefunction() and potential potential() will be exported. In this system, the model is generated by RigidRotor and several parameters m_1, m_2, R and hbar are set as optional arguments.\n\nusing Antique\nRR = RigidRotor(m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, R=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nParameters:\n\nRR.m_1\nRR.m_2\nRR.R\nRR.hbar\n\nEigenvalues:\n\nenergy(RR, l=0)\nenergy(RR, l=1)\nenergy(RR, l=2)\n\nWave functions:\n\nwavefunction(RR, 0, 0, l=2, m=1)\nwavefunction(RR, π/4, 0, l=2, m=1)\nwavefunction(RR, π/4, π/2, l=2, m=1)\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure(size=(420,300))\nax = PolarAxis(f[1,1], title=L\"$\\theta\\mapsto|\\psi_{2,1}(\\theta,0)|^2$\", rticklabelsvisible=false, thetalimits=(0,pi))\nlines!(ax, 0..2pi, θ->abs(wavefunction(RR,θ,0,l=2,m=1))^2, linewidth=2)\n\nf\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nf = Figure(size=(420,300))\nax = PolarAxis(f[1,1], title=L\"$\\theta\\mapsto|\\psi_{lm}(\\theta,0)|^2$\", rticklabelsvisible=false, thetalimits=(0,pi))\nl1 = lines!(ax, 0..2pi, θ->abs(wavefunction(RR,θ,0,l=0,m=0))^2, linewidth=2)\nl2 = lines!(ax, 0..2pi, θ->abs(wavefunction(RR,θ,0,l=1,m=0))^2, linewidth=2)\nl3 = lines!(ax, 0..2pi, θ->abs(wavefunction(RR,θ,0,l=1,m=1))^2, linewidth=2)\nl4 = lines!(ax, 0..2pi, θ->abs(wavefunction(RR,θ,0,l=2,m=1))^2, linewidth=2)\nLegend(f[2,1], [l1,l2,l3,l4], [L\"(l,m)=(0,0)\",L\"(1,0)\",L\"(1,1)\",L\"(2,1)\"], framevisible=false, orientation=:horizontal, tellwidth=false, tellheight=true)\n\nf\nsave(\"assets/fig/RigidRotor.png\", f) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"RigidRotor/#Testing","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"Unit testing and Integration testing were done using a computer algebra system (Symbolics.jl) and numerical integration (QuadGK.jl). The test script is here.\n\nusing Markdown\nusing Antique\nMarkdown.parse(Base.read(\"../../test/result/RigidRotor.md\", String))","title":"Testing"},{"category":"type","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.RigidRotors.RigidRotor-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"Model\n\nThis model is described with the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n  hatH psi(thetavarphi) = E psi(thetavarphi)\n\nand the Hamiltonian\n\nbeginaligned\n  hatH = - frachbar^22mu nabla^2 + V(r) \n          = - frachbar^22I left frac1sintheta fracpartialpartialtheta left(sintheta fracpartialpartialthetaright) + frac1sin^2theta fracpartial^2partialphi^2  right \n          = fracL^22I\nendaligned\n\nwhere I=mu R^2 is the moment of intertia, mu=left(frac1m_1+frac1m_2right)^-1 is the reduced mass of two particles, R is the distance between the two particles, and L^2 is the angular momentum operator. Parameters are specified with the following struct:\n\nRR = RigidRotor(m_1=1.0, m_2=1.0, R=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n\nm₁ and m₂ are mass of two particles, R is the distance, and hbar is the reduced Planck constant (Dirac's constant).\n\nReferences\n\n[10] D. A. McQuarrie, J. D. Simon, Physical chemistry : a molecular approach (University Science Books, 1997) (https://mitpress.mit.edu/9781940380216/physical-chemistry/) p.173, 5.8 The Energy Levels of a Rigid Rotator Are E = hbar^2 J(J+1)  2I\n[18] Chemistry Libre Texts, (https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/PhysicalandTheoreticalChemistryTextbookMaps/PhysicalChemistry(LibreTexts)/05%3ATheHarmonicOscillatorandtheRigidRotor/5.08%3ATheEnergyLevelsofaRigid_Rotor)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.RigidRotors.RigidRotor"},{"category":"method","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.potential-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any}-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"potential(model::RigidRotor, r)\n\nbeginaligned\n  V(r)\n  = 0\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.potential"},{"category":"method","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.energy-Tuple{RigidRotor}-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"energy(model::RigidRotor; l::Integer=0)\n\nE_l\n= frachbar^22Il(l+1)\n\nwhere I=mu R^2 is the moment of inertia, R is the distance, and mu is the reduced mass of the two particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.energy"},{"category":"method","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.wavefunction-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any, Any}-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"wavefunction(model::RigidRotor, θ, φ; l::Integer=0, m::Integer=0)\n\npsi_lm(thetavarphi) = Y_lm(thetavarphi)\n\nThe wave functions are the spherical harmonics. The domain is 0leq theta lt pi and 0leq varphi lt 2pi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.wavefunction"},{"category":"method","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.spherical_harmonic-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any, Any}-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"spherical_harmonic(model::RigidRotor, θ, φ; l=0, m=0)\n\nY_lm(thetavarphi) = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac2l+14pi frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m (costheta) mathrme^imvarphi\n\nThe domain is 0leq theta lt pi 0leq varphi lt 2pi. Note that some variants are connected by \n\ni^m+m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^fracm+m2 sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m = (-1)^m sqrtfrac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"method","location":"RigidRotor/#Antique.legendre_polynomial-Tuple{RigidRotor, Any}-RigidRotor","page":"Rigid Rotor","text":"legendre_polynomial(model::RigidRotor, x; n=0, m=0)\n\nnote: Note\nThe closed-form expression has been verified to be numerically stable up to n=10. For larger n, numerical instabilities may arise; a recurrence-relation implementation will address this limitation.\n\nRodrigues' formula & closed-form:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_n^m(x)\n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m P_n(x) \n  = left( 1-x^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^mmathrmdx^m frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right \n  = frac12^n (1-x^2)^m2 sum_j=0^leftlfloorfracn-m2rightrfloor (-1)^j frac(2n-2j)j (n-j) (n-2j-m) x^(n-2j-m)\nendaligned\n\nwhere Legendre polynomials are defined as P_n(x) = frac12^n n fracmathrmd^nmathrmdx ^n left left( x^2-1 right)^n right. Note that P_l^-m = (-1)^m frac(l-m)(l+m) P_l^m for m0. (It is not compatible with P_k^m(t) = (-1)^mleft( 1-t^2 right)^m2 fracmathrmd^m P_k(t)mathrmdt^m caused by (-1)^m.) The specific formulae are given below.\n\nExamples:\n\nbeginaligned\n  P_0^0(x) = 1 \n  P_1^0(x) = x \n  P_1^1(x) = left(+1right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^0(x) = -12 + 32 x^2 \n  P_2^1(x) = left(-3 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_2^2(x) = 3 - 6 x \n  P_3^0(x) = -32 x + 52 x^3 \n  P_3^1(x) = left(32 - 152 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_3^2(x) = 15 x - 30 x^2 \n  P_3^3(x) = left(15 - 30 xright)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^0(x) = 38 - 154 x^2 + 358 x^4 \n  P_4^1(x) = left(- 152 x + 352 x^3right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^2(x) = -152 + 15 x + 1052 x^2 - 105 x^3 \n  P_4^3(x) = left(105 x - 210 x^2right)sqrt1-x^2 \n  P_4^4(x) = 105 - 420 x + 420 x^2 \n   vdots\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Antique.legendre_polynomial"},{"category":"section","location":"#Antique.jl","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Build Status)  (Image: Stable) (Image: Dev)\n\nAntique.jl provides self-contained, well-tested, and well-documented implementations of analytical solutions to solvable quantum mechanical models. Analytical solutions serve as reliable test oracles for software testing in the development of numerical methods. In addition to testing numerical methods, this package is also useful for teaching quantum mechanics. We aim to support researchers, lecturers, students, and anyone interested in quantum mechanics.","title":"Antique.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"Run the following code on the REPL to install this package.\n\n]add Antique@0.15.0\n\nOr run import Pkg; Pkg.add(; name=\"Antique\", version=\"0.15.0\") to install on Jupyter Notebook. The version of this package can be found at ]status Antique or import Pkg; Pkg.status(\"Antique\").","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#Usage-and-Examples","page":"Home","text":"Install Antique.jl for the first use and run using Antique before each use.\n\nusing Antique\n\nThe three main functions are energy(model; ...) for the energy E, potential(model, ...) for the potential V, and wavefunction(model, ...; ...) for the wave function psi. They are exported by using Antique. To avoid name conflicts, run import Antique and qualify them as Antique.energy(), Antique.potential(), and Antique.wavefunction(), or import only the names you need, for example using Antique: potential, energy, wavefunction, HarmonicOscillator.\n\nFirst, create a harmonic oscillator with unit force constant, mass, and reduced Planck constant.\n\nHO = HarmonicOscillator(k=1.0, m=1.0, hbar=1.0)\n; #hide\n\nThe model parameters can be accessed as properties.\n\nHO.k\nHO.m\nHO.hbar\n\nUse energy to calculate the energy of each state specified by the quantum number n.\n\nenergy(HO, n=0)\nenergy(HO, n=1)\nenergy(HO, n=2)\n\nUse potential to evaluate the harmonic potential at a position x.\n\npotential(HO, 0.0)\npotential(HO, 1.0)\npotential(HO, 2.0)\n\nUse wavefunction to evaluate a wave function for a state n at a position x.\n\nwavefunction(HO, 0.0, n=0)\nwavefunction(HO, 0.0, n=1)\nwavefunction(HO, 1.0, n=1)\nwavefunction(HO, 100.0, n=1)\n\nThere are more examples on each model page.","title":"Usage & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-Models","page":"Home","text":"<div class=\"catalog\">\n  <div class=\"item\">\n    <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"./InfinitePotentialWell\">\n      <img src=\"assets/fig/InfinitePotentialWell.png\" alt=\"InfinitePotentialWell\"/>\n    </a>\n    <code>InfinitePotentialWell</code>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"item\">\n    <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"./HarmonicOscillator\">\n      <img src=\"assets/fig/HarmonicOscillator.png\" alt=\"HarmonicOscillator\"/>\n    </a>\n    <code>HarmonicOscillator</code>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"item\">\n    <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"./PoschlTeller\">\n      <img src=\"assets/fig/PoschlTeller.png\" alt=\"PoschlTeller\"/>\n    </a>\n    <code>PoschlTeller</code>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"item\">\n    <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"./MorsePotential\">\n      <img src=\"assets/fig/MorsePotential.png\" alt=\"MorsePotential\"/>\n    </a>\n    <code>MorsePotential</code>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\nDelta Potential DeltaPotential\nInfinite Potential Well InfinitePotentialWell\nHarmonic Oscillator HarmonicOscillator\nPoschlTeller PoschlTeller\nMorse Potential MorsePotential\nRigid Rotor RigidRotor\nInfinite PotentialWell 3D InfinitePotentialWell3D\nSpherical Oscillator SphericalOscillator\nHydrogen Atom HydrogenAtom\nCoulomb 2-Body System CoulombTwoBody","title":"Supported Models"},{"category":"section","location":"#Demonstration","page":"Home","text":"This is an example of a variational calculation for the hydrogen atom based on Thijssen(2007). We check the accuracy of the numerical solution by comparison with the analytical solution. Comparing wave functions can be difficult, but Antique.jl makes it easy. You can extend it to excited states (n1) as well as the ground state (n=1). Thus, Antique.jl is useful for testing numerical methods. We hope many numerical methods to be developed using Antique.jl.\n\n# calculations based on Thijssen(2007) https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139171397\nusing LinearAlgebra\nα = [13.00773, 1.962079, 0.444529, 0.1219492] \nnₘₐₓ = length(α)\nS = [(pi/(α[i]+α[j]))^(3/2) for i=1:nₘₐₓ, j=1:nₘₐₓ]\nH = [3*pi^(3/2)*α[i]*α[j]/(α[i]+α[j])^(5/2) - 2*pi/(α[i]+α[j]) for i=1:nₘₐₓ, j=1:nₘₐₓ]\nE, C = eigen(Symmetric(H),Symmetric(S))\n\n# norm & energy\nimport Antique\nHA = Antique.HydrogenAtom(Z=1, E_h=1.0, a_0=1.0, m_e=1.0, hbar=1.0)\nprintln(\"Norm\")\nprintln(\"  numerical : \", transpose(C[:,1]) * S * C[:,1])\nprintln(\"  analytical: \", 1)\nprintln(\"Energy\")\nprintln(\"  numerical : \", E[1])\nprintln(\"  analytical: \", Antique.energy(HA,n=1))\n\n# wave function\nusing CairoMakie\nfig = Figure(size=(420,300), fontsize=11, backgroundcolor=:transparent)\naxis = Axis(fig[1,1], xlabel=L\"$r$\", ylabel=L\"$\\psi(r,0,0)$\", limits=(0,4,0,0.6), ylabelsize=16.5, xlabelsize=16.5)\nlines!(axis, 0:0.01:10, r -> sum(C[:,1] .* exp.(-α*r^2)), label=\"Numerical, Thijssen(2007)\")\nlines!(axis, 0:0.01:10, r -> real(Antique.wavefunction(HA,r,0,0)), color=:black, linestyle=:dash, label=\"Analytical, Antique.jl\")\naxislegend(axis, position=:rt, framevisible=false)\nfig\nsave(\"assets/fig/demonstration.png\", fig) # hide\n; # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Demonstration"},{"category":"section","location":"#Future-Works","page":"Home","text":"The candidate models are listed on the Wikipedia page of List of quantum-mechanical systems with analytical solutions. Please submit your requests and suggestions as issues on GitHub.","title":"Future Works"},{"category":"section","location":"#Developer-Guide","page":"Home","text":"Here is the guideline for adding new models.","title":"Developer Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgment","page":"Home","text":"Thanks to all contributors. This package was named by @KB-satou and @ultimatile. @MartinMikkelsen contributed to writing docstrings. Special thanks to @hyrodium for his help with managing the documentation and advice on coding style. @lhapp27 implemented 2 models, and @ajarifi implemented 3 models.","title":"Acknowledgment"}]
}
